Part 3. Apply the official Mosaic AI custom-schema contract
The official Databricks notebook uses wrap_output to convert LangGraph output into the recommended chat-completion-compatible output format, including custom_outputs. 
Databricks


For your agent, define the following output conversion:
Cell 9 — Define the output adapter
def wrap_output(result):    final_message = result["messages"][-1]    return {        "messages": [            {                "role": "assistant",                "content": final_message.content            }        ],        "custom_outputs": result.get("custom_outputs", {})    }wrapped_result = wrap_output(agent_result)print(json.dumps(wrapped_result, indent=2))



The purpose of this step is to keep the chat response and the structured rule output separate.
- messages: readable response for the caller.
- custom_outputs: structured final rule, status and version.
Important technical distinction: This is the output adaptation step, not yet proof that MLflow can log and serve the model. The official model signature and model-loading test below provide the next validation.
Part 4. Prepare the agent for MLflow model logging
The official approach uses a two-notebook, models-as-code workflow. The agent notebook defines the model; the driver notebook logs, registers and deploys it. 
Databricks


For the first compatibility check, we can log the compiled LangGraph object directly through MLflow's LangChain flavor. This avoids pretending that your current notebook's in-memory spark session will automatically exist in a deployed model.
Cell 10 — Create the model signature
from mlflow.models.signature import ModelSignatureagent_signature = ModelSignature(    inputs=CHAT_MODEL_INPUT_SCHEMA,    outputs=CHAT_MODEL_OUTPUT_SCHEMA)print("Mosaic AI chat schema loaded!")print(agent_signature)



Cell 11 — Log the LangGraph model
with mlflow.start_run():    logged_agent = mlflow.langchain.log_model(        lc_model=agent,        artifact_path="rule_authoring_agent",        signature=agent_signature    )print("Model logged successfully!")print("Model URI:", logged_agent.model_uri)



This is an important validation step. If the LangChain flavor cannot accept your compiled graph, or the signature does not match, you will see the actual error here.

Note that this is an object-based logging test. The official notebook's full models-as-code implementation uses an agent source notebook and a separate driver notebook. We will use that source-file approach when preparing a deployable artifact.
Part 5. Register the model in Unity Catalog
You have:
- Catalog: workspace
- Schema: samples
Use the following code:
Cell 12 — Register
mlflow.set_registry_uri("databricks-uc")model_name = "workspace.samples.rule_authoring_agent"registered_model = mlflow.register_model(    model_uri=logged_agent.model_uri,    name=model_name)print("Registered model:", model_name)print("Version:", registered_model.version)



If this gives a permissions error, it means your current trial identity cannot create registered models in that schema. It does not mean LangGraph is incompatible.
Part 6. Deployment — optional for your current task
The official driver notebook uses the Databricks Agents deployment API:
from databricks import agentsdeployment = agents.deploy(    model_name,    registered_model.version)print(deployment)




